#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119574 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/id #uuid "3e36e916-16af-55eb-9ece-026b68eb93ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3338" :question/problem "Consider a curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $\\mathbf r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "7f4861fe-a783-5874-a22b-b4644257ae0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3339" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at the point $\\mathbf{r}(0),$ if the curve and the derivative $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t, \\:\\dfrac{ t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf T'(t)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{(t^2+1)^3}}\\left\\langle -t, \\:  1\\right\\rangle \\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ and the derivative $\\mathbf T'(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula to compute the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\mathbf \\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(0),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨1,t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate $\\mathbf T'(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(1+{0}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1+{0}^{2})^{3}}}⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & = \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩+1\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/cc/cc9e1eaf1dc8655565a94b7ef0146dc2bb458cc7376828b19352acc01ddbeaad.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119576 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "8d58f131-7260-5346-9a88-60862c0a1b55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3340" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle e^{ t}, \\: e^{- t} \\right\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $\\mathbf r(t),$ we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(0),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(0)$ and $\\mathbf r''(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,-1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}=\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60712d5b-cdb0-55d3-a4f8-50942ccd11f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444670} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ee4cdf-998e-5716-aef1-4bb01bec2434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444676} {:answer/id #uuid "bac1618d-71c7-5ccf-9a5d-6130f69bfae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444678} {:answer/id #uuid "bec816be-742c-59d2-855d-77a5f733bd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444679} {:answer/id #uuid "c3741ba0-1c15-5491-b929-38f45abef427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bec816be-742c-59d2-855d-77a5f733bd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444679} :answer-field/id #uuid "40148947-347b-5613-b0f0-048b08f6395d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6116bb62-b350-5926-8756-9fc43f77db50" :question/math-academy-id "q-132107" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}))}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{16})}=16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1954be8d-5088-5b12-9c72-4ea1a2d470ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263240} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3c1426-12b5-5876-83d9-40109307170f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264227} {:answer/id #uuid "bff3195d-ef11-5c9b-b5cf-bfe153a1a200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264670} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c34ea5-cf33-551a-9b54-f46e4db5cbec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264747} {:answer/id #uuid "d752747e-ec49-5493-9d07-78d09354418b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7c34ea5-cf33-551a-9b54-f46e4db5cbec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264747} :answer-field/id #uuid "32a9a4f2-4b90-5fe5-adb5-339e386dab38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "700d118c-0927-554d-9106-fd23a8f63322" :question/math-academy-id "q-132112" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $r(\\pi )$ if the curve and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $r(\\pi )$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17dfe97c-105a-5dbd-a5d8-f67bff74a3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444661} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6dafae-a662-56d5-adac-629f556d6830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444680} {:answer/id #uuid "c8aa7bd2-92e2-5691-ad30-ce9f13f06fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444683} {:answer/id #uuid "daaccc09-6276-5373-8cc7-35d3a431e7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444685} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe72f53-9ff7-54c2-8de2-57f21faf388d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbe72f53-9ff7-54c2-8de2-57f21faf388d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444686} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dfff41f-ad79-57e1-bbe0-18110bfed6e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "577c86ab-d1fb-551c-9998-1ea74c39e37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132119" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=2$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(2),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(2) & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(2)$ and ${r}^{″}(2)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2) & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(2) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(2){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}})}^{3}}{(\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{27}{16\\sqrt{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a76e0c-15ea-5619-9380-8cd2dd826b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263224} {:answer/id #uuid "34aa0d05-2d91-5b25-bdf5-9dd393e0d077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263462} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac5f39b-1a2b-5398-907e-16caf73406a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263917} {:answer/id #uuid "b00ad2c4-1d5f-52df-bab0-51e1378bf277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264518} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1845cb-b069-541a-844e-89b1bf20e6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34aa0d05-2d91-5b25-bdf5-9dd393e0d077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263462} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee21c8f8-42a5-5d83-b2db-74b922cf4739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf0c275d-135e-528b-bd00-cb3abdf966eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132130" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343bc97a-3f6b-52b9-83d8-ee3bcc396c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{5}{3}⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444665} {:answer/id #uuid "56d2d7f8-0ae6-569f-883d-0ab6f7d76ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{5}{9}⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444668} {:answer/id #uuid "84c1582a-9c55-52d8-bdb8-f1855dfee339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{3}⟩$ and the radius is $4\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444672} {:answer/id #uuid "935d3fcb-4001-59af-9ebd-2e9f785033b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{10}{3}⟩$ and the radius is $4\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444673} {:answer/id #uuid "b7dff845-3395-5778-ba65-c9c3d11732df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-\\frac{9}{4},\\frac{5}{9}⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "935d3fcb-4001-59af-9ebd-2e9f785033b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{10}{3}⟩$ and the radius is $4\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444673} :answer-field/id #uuid "a97de606-d3e0-57f4-9214-46ea151db54d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "455e62c8-3b81-5de7-9ef0-5fb64015c1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132206" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3})$ where $t=1,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{t}{t+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(1)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3}⟩,$ since the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3})$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{\\frac{t}{t+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}(t+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2(t+1)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}(1+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2(1+1)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2(2)^{3/2}},-\\frac{1}{2(2)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=1$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)+R(1)N(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3}⟩+4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3}⟩+4⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3}⟩+⟨4,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{10}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be3fe6bc-31a3-571a-9418-f7fc505803cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118072} {:answer/id #uuid "05f309a4-26df-565e-b89d-f3b6ebff2211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118073} {:answer/id #uuid "1722b14d-e4e2-57cb-b7e2-249bb981fc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118074} {:answer/id #uuid "225ee875-19fe-53df-b5e4-9774431ca64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118075} {:answer/id #uuid "76ae31ac-87dd-5e7c-a0a1-c4fda59c4559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "225ee875-19fe-53df-b5e4-9774431ca64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118075} :answer-field/id #uuid "494f239d-944f-5831-a74b-2f3c116ace88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d3effa7-89be-50df-bb8d-20f0a929d59d" :question/math-academy-id "q-34319" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}+(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+9{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+25{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5732a0-d981-581b-bed3-e658d886cdb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1,0)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317661} {:answer/id #uuid "3aad45a8-b349-54d3-9a1d-d7e6a55c1789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1,0)$ and the radius is $\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317754} {:answer/id #uuid "591f3088-c0f6-5f09-8428-ea4ebf479939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1,-\\pi )$ and the radius is $\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317867} {:answer/id #uuid "8dadc1f8-5608-58e2-86fe-62d6e0a688ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1,-2\\pi )$ and the radius is $\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318044} {:answer/id #uuid "efcd825d-266c-5ffd-a9e5-69b4061e3d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1,-2\\pi )$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aad45a8-b349-54d3-9a1d-d7e6a55c1789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1,0)$ and the radius is $\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317754} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d7a6c3a-6be8-5e35-9a64-ce8b55e5a19a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b843aaf-f0c5-5942-abb0-c6ab73ca4229" :question/math-academy-id "q-34624" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(-1,-\\pi )$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=\\pi ,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨-1,-\\pi ⟩$,  since the point $P(-1,-\\pi )$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{\\pi }{1}=\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,-\\pi ⟩+\\pi ⟨0,1⟩=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f3d1a5-cc66-58c7-8d6e-788bb6fcaa44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319414} {:answer/id #uuid "126cbfbb-8e1e-563f-ba80-0b64161e037e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319441} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7f074c-ab27-5693-814e-e9bacc82dab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319722} {:answer/id #uuid "ed30e9d2-6e3f-5bc9-a100-e30acb50f09f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320102} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c7b1ac-53b1-5fb7-aa3a-dcdefd9568ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09f3d1a5-cc66-58c7-8d6e-788bb6fcaa44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319414} :answer-field/id #uuid "30a98473-0d64-5b42-aa20-fce4bd16a581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f89d804c-5610-5e9d-94e2-86a0ad458afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-35114" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "369d7dc4-1f2c-58c7-8955-91cabe185acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333492} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbb8b9b-d6a9-5997-bce5-90424ff1180f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333979} {:answer/id #uuid "b24b6fea-adc3-54a3-94dd-ad481dc00180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334555} {:answer/id #uuid "e0405203-826a-52d8-8a79-dcf3a04354bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334940} {:answer/id #uuid "fa731375-053c-5708-bf47-16a574de2b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "369d7dc4-1f2c-58c7-8955-91cabe185acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333492} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc84e1e-1ce0-5ed5-b29d-dc063985cbbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6d0a779-de60-5fed-8e29-8de76943c6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-64732" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{9})}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13837c57-fcf6-59ed-a939-a470108db450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333161} {:answer/id #uuid "66df2533-6415-54e6-aecd-02e34294c2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333928} {:answer/id #uuid "9199da41-115a-549a-926f-7560fc8fae17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334288} {:answer/id #uuid "af3ba4c9-eaee-5458-a599-2c97ee46fb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334534} {:answer/id #uuid "e32c6e4d-94ff-5ce6-869c-2bf3aead8861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13837c57-fcf6-59ed-a939-a470108db450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333161} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ac7cd9a-bd4e-577f-9519-5227a1ee21af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85d6e3bb-1239-5725-994c-6f104c6ddc40" :question/math-academy-id "q-64738" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}(-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{8}{25})}=\\frac{25}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0159ae1f-8d8b-505c-91bb-33e3cb94ce88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,-1)$ and the radius is $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119104} {:answer/id #uuid "18864349-f844-598b-8bd8-2499081b262c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,0)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444662} {:answer/id #uuid "26713c2f-51c0-5113-aace-e97dae2a9d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444664} {:answer/id #uuid "c21a2526-c8f2-5cf5-8dba-ef6702413f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,2)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444681} {:answer/id #uuid "d94db2da-7382-5233-8016-048bd89a7acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c21a2526-c8f2-5cf5-8dba-ef6702413f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,2)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444681} :answer-field/id #uuid "02dc88d7-02c9-503f-943d-b4979f80536a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69b5cf6a-1be8-58cf-9f82-85a803f2002d" :question/math-academy-id "q-64839" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1)$ where $t=0$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,1⟩,$ since the point $P(0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖$: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2}+1)^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2}+1)^{3}}}⟩=⟨0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨0,1⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,1⟩+1⟨0,1⟩=⟨0,2⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7c676a-8ada-5b80-8687-e725bb845066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(2,0)$ and the radius $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444659} {:answer/id #uuid "160ecfc6-2535-55df-b403-21e3404605cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(3,1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444660} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7e95d1-a185-5c36-941f-f42bb0377bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{3}{2},1)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444666} {:answer/id #uuid "52439b04-8154-53ed-8e04-90bf6ccb85dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-1,1)$ and the radius $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444667} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb242ab-2a10-5e82-bf8e-855144702e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7e95d1-a185-5c36-941f-f42bb0377bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{3}{2},1)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444666} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ed9b22-e11c-5770-9922-e0f3dca90fb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21c086f0-0661-5c6d-8a42-5fe15673cf33" :question/math-academy-id "q-97706" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is \n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,1⟩,$ since the point $P(1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(1+4{t}^{2})^{3}}},\\,-\\frac{4t}{\\sqrt{(1+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(1+4(0)^{2})^{3}}},\\,-\\frac{4(0)}{\\sqrt{(1+4(0)^{2})^{3}}}⟩=⟨2,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}⟨2,0⟩=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,1⟩+\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,0⟩=⟨\\frac{3}{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8215fc14-563c-5e9c-b543-3b181532b76e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,-1)$ and the radius $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404935} {:answer/id #uuid "42923454-cf65-5ffa-b13a-8454aab9fce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404936} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4044bf-8190-5ffe-8ef7-3daae644117b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,\\pi )$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404937} {:answer/id #uuid "4380c049-b003-5a9e-9947-934c39c62143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404938} {:answer/id #uuid "28cddacf-ab92-5751-b040-402638871db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4380c049-b003-5a9e-9947-934c39c62143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404938} :answer-field/id #uuid "77606255-ab40-56b2-a453-f732ee8398aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd2936d9-6a40-5af8-901d-a96142d64a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-97720" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,4)$ where $t=1,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖=3\\pi ,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2})⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t),$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(1)=⟨0,4⟩,$  since the point $P(0,4)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2}),-\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =⟨-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨0,-\\pi ⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-\\pi )^{2}}=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{3\\pi }{\\pi }=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=1$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }⟨0,-\\pi ⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)+R(1)N(1) & =⟨0,4⟩+3⟨0,-1⟩=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397fefec-25e9-51df-956e-4d4b55444186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361098} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea68c89-9002-5f07-8a7c-b297b7adc902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361395} {:answer/id #uuid "c10febe1-3c66-57e0-b62d-808330b25e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361702} {:answer/id #uuid "c5aff563-951e-5537-9949-923b00386848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361732} {:answer/id #uuid "d318e5b2-6b3f-531d-a4fc-c635478aeb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5aff563-951e-5537-9949-923b00386848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361732} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef995365-0b4c-502d-9aad-9595d82e6b2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5692f221-3e0b-5059-a8e0-07dee6b9fc2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98708" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=4$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨4\\sqrt{t-3},t-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(4)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(4)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t-3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}},1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(4) & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(t-3)^{3}}},0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(4)$ and ${r}^{″}(4)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(4) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(4){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=5\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2224ac84-3f36-51f0-b57f-7543c32e2f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444663} {:answer/id #uuid "63e39650-4998-587f-bb67-a3e3b9b1823e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444671} {:answer/id #uuid "997d42bb-b84a-5f7f-8a34-f6035e4d200f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444674} {:answer/id #uuid "9d346ea8-2679-5e9c-813d-0909d9f6e1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444675} {:answer/id #uuid "fee16127-fffd-5793-b350-764f5e703dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e39650-4998-587f-bb67-a3e3b9b1823e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444671} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fbeb716-f779-5cae-a88d-6239351ca62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e82dd54-dc76-5073-8b6d-fd7d44dc815e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98717" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨0,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨-4,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-16\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{{4}^{3}}{\\,16\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
